## URI Versioning in Spring

```java
// Strategy 1: Version in path
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/orders")
public class OrderControllerV1 {
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderResponseV1 getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return v1Mapper.toResponse(orderService.findById(id));
    }
}

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v2/orders")
public class OrderControllerV2 {
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderResponseV2 getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return v2Mapper.toResponse(orderService.findById(id));
        // V2 might add new fields, restructure, rename properties
    }
}

// If V2 is mostly the same: extend or delegate
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v2/orders")
public class OrderControllerV2 extends OrderControllerV1 {
    // Override only the methods that changed
    @Override
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderResponseV2 getOrder(@PathVariable Long id) { ... }
    // Inherits unchanged endpoints from V1
}
```

## Header Versioning

```java
// Strategy 2: Version in Accept header
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
public class OrderController {

    @GetMapping(
        value = "/{id}",
        produces = "application/vnd.myapi.v1+json"
    )
    public OrderResponseV1 getOrderV1(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return v1Mapper.toResponse(orderService.findById(id));
    }

    @GetMapping(
        value = "/{id}",
        produces = "application/vnd.myapi.v2+json"
    )
    public OrderResponseV2 getOrderV2(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return v2Mapper.toResponse(orderService.findById(id));
    }

    // Client sends: Accept: application/vnd.myapi.v2+json
    // Spring selects matching produces
}
```

## Request Header (Custom Header)

```java
// Strategy 3: Custom API version header
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
public class OrderController {

    @GetMapping("/{id}", headers = "API-Version=1")
    public OrderResponseV1 getOrderV1(@PathVariable Long id) { ... }

    @GetMapping("/{id}", headers = "API-Version=2")
    public OrderResponseV2 getOrderV2(@PathVariable Long id) { ... }

    // Client sends: API-Version: 2
}
```

## Versioning with API Gateway

```yaml
# In API Gateway (e.g., Kong, AWS API Gateway):
# Route rules:
routes:
  - name: orders-v1
    paths: ["/api/v1/orders"]
    service: orders-service
    strip_path: true
    plugins:
      - name: request-transformer
        config:
          add:
            headers: ["API-Version: 1"]

  - name: orders-v2
    paths: ["/api/v2/orders"]
    service: orders-service
    strip_path: true
    plugins:
      - name: request-transformer
        config:
          add:
            headers: ["API-Version: 2"]

# Internally, all services use header versioning
# Gateway translates URI version to header for internal routing
```

## Breaking vs Non-Breaking Changes

```
Non-breaking (safe — no version bump needed):
  + Adding new optional fields to response
  + Adding new optional request parameters
  + Adding new endpoints
  + Relaxing validation (accepting more values)

Breaking (requires new version):
  - Removing or renaming response fields
  - Changing field types (string → number)
  - Making previously optional field required
  - Changing HTTP status codes for existing operations
  - Removing endpoints
  - Changing URI structure

// Design principle: Postel's Law
// Be conservative in what you send, liberal in what you accept
// Accept old field names for backward compat:
public class CreateOrderRequest {
    @JsonProperty("userId")
    @JsonAlias({"user_id", "customerId"})  // Accept old names too
    private Long userId;
}
```

## Deprecation Strategy

```java
// Inform clients of deprecation via headers:
@GetMapping
@Deprecated
public ResponseEntity<List<OrderResponseV1>> listOrdersV1() {
    return ResponseEntity.ok()
        .header("Deprecation", "true")
        .header("Sunset", "Wed, 01 Jan 2025 00:00:00 GMT")  // When it goes away
        .header("Link", "</api/v2/orders>; rel=\"successor-version\"")
        .body(orderService.findAll().stream().map(v1Mapper::toResponse).collect(toList()));
}
// RFC 8594 defines Deprecation and Sunset headers
// Clients that handle these can warn developers to upgrade
```

## Production Reality

URI versioning with `/api/v1/` prefix is the practical choice for most APIs. Version at the resource level, not the operation level — all endpoints under `/api/v2/` share the same contract. Set a deprecation and sunset date before removing old versions, communicate it clearly in documentation and response headers, and stick to the date — API consumers plan migrations around your communicated timelines.

## Jackson Backward Compatibility

```java
// Accept both old and new field names during migration:
public class UserResponse {
    // Old name: 'name' → New name: 'fullName'
    // Keep both working:
    @JsonProperty("fullName")          // Primary (new) name in response
    @JsonAlias({"name", "full_name"})  // Also accept these in requests
    private String fullName;

    // Deprecated field: still include in response but marked
    @JsonProperty("phone")
    @Deprecated
    private String phone;  // Will be removed in v2; new field is 'phoneNumber'

    @JsonProperty("phoneNumber")  // New canonical name
    private String phoneNumber;
}

// Client receives both 'phone' and 'phoneNumber' — time to migrate
// New clients: use 'phoneNumber'
// Old clients: still get 'phone' until removed
```

## Jackson Configuration for Strict/Lenient Clients

```java
// Server-side: Don't fail if client sends unknown fields
@JsonIgnoreProperties(ignoreUnknown = true)
public class CreateOrderRequest {
    // If client sends extra field 'legacyField' — ignored, no error
}

// Global Jackson config:
@Bean
public ObjectMapper objectMapper() {
    return Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder.json()
        .featuresToDisable(
            DeserializationFeature.FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES  // Ignore extra fields
        )
        .featuresToEnable(
            SerializationFeature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS  // Or disable for ISO-8601
        )
        .build();
}
```

## Consumer-Driven Contract Testing with Pact

```java
// Consumer (client) side — defines expectations:
@ExtendWith(PactConsumerTestExt.class)
@PactTestFor(providerName = "OrderService")
public class OrderServiceContractTest {

    @Pact(consumer = "WebApp")
    public RequestResponsePact getOrderContract(PactDslWithProvider builder) {
        return builder
            .given("Order 42 exists")
            .uponReceiving("GET order 42")
                .method("GET")
                .path("/api/orders/42")
            .willRespondWith()
                .status(200)
                .body(new PactDslJsonBody()
                    .integerType("id", 42)
                    .stringType("status", "PENDING")
                    .decimalType("total", 99.99)
                    // Client doesn't care about other fields
                )
            .toPact();
    }

    @Test
    @PactTestFor(pactMethod = "getOrderContract")
    void verifyOrderContract(MockServer mockServer) {
        // Test against mock server — verifies client works with contract
        OrderClient client = new OrderClient(mockServer.getUrl());
        Order order = client.getOrder(42L);
        assertThat(order.getStatus()).isEqualTo("PENDING");
    }
}

// Provider (server) side:
@Provider("OrderService")
@PactBroker(url = "https://pact-broker.company.com")
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
public class OrderServicePactVerificationTest {
    // Runs all consumer contracts against the real service
    // Build fails if any consumer contract is broken
}
```

## Additive Change Strategy

```java
// Safe: add optional field to response
// V1 response: {id, status, total}
// V2 response: {id, status, total, items, createdAt}  ← New fields added
// V1 clients: ignore new fields automatically

// Safe: add optional field to request
public class CreateOrderRequest {
    @NotNull
    private Long userId;
    // ... existing fields ...

    // New optional field — V1 clients don't send it, default applies:
    @Size(max = 100)
    private String note;  // = null if not provided
}

// Unsafe: remove field (breaking)
// Unsafe: rename field without alias (breaking)
// Unsafe: change type string→number (breaking)
// Unsafe: make optional field required (breaking)
```

## Production Reality

Invest in contract testing before you have multiple API consumers — retrofitting is painful. The contract test pyramid: unit tests for business logic, contract tests for API compatibility, a few end-to-end tests for critical user journeys. Contract tests run on every PR and block merges that break existing consumer contracts. This gives you confidence to evolve APIs quickly without fear of breaking partners.
